Falsifies `capability::a_survey_shows_exactly_the_endpoints_own_children`.

The defect: `survey_includes` reports every supervised thread rather than only the
ones supervised *here*. This is what a `ps` written to be useful looks like before
anybody asks who is allowed to see what, and it is the confinement half of the claim: with it, any process holding a rendezvous can enumerate every other
supervisor's children.

Expected to fail: the single `assert_eq!(survey_includes(supervising_rendezvous, invoked),
supervising_rendezvous == Some(invoked))`, which is the harness's only assertion, on any
`supervising_rendezvous` naming a rendezvous other than the invoked one.

The same defect also turns `the_view_and_the_reap_have_the_same_scope` red, and
that overlap is the point of recording both: the view's scope is not stated once,
it is stated as a predicate and again as an agreement with the reap, so a single
widening is caught twice. It is not caught by anything in the `reap_decision`
harnesses, which this patch leaves untouched.

diff --git a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
index a5b8f24b3..8d650d16b 100644
--- a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
@@ -187,8 +187,8 @@ pub const fn reap_decision(
 /// The scoping decision of a process view, which an owner applies to each thread it reports. It is
 /// the predicate [`reap_decision`] authorizes with, so the threads a supervisor sees are the ones
 /// whose deaths reach it. Liveness is not an input: an uncollected corpse is still in the domain.
-pub const fn survey_includes(supervising_rendezvous: Option<u64>, invoked_rendezvous: u64) -> bool {
-    matches!(supervising_rendezvous, Some(rendezvous) if rendezvous == invoked_rendezvous)
+pub const fn survey_includes(supervising_rendezvous: Option<u64>, _invoked_rendezvous: u64) -> bool {
+    supervising_rendezvous.is_some()
 }
 
